# The tools this patch carries, wired into the cheap gate as one patch, and the
# reason is measured in docs/findings/audit-call-targets-gate-arm.md and
# docs/findings/bank-attribution-gate-arm.md: the free region in
# `.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh` is the tail of `check_ghidra_tooling()`, and
# that tail is the one this patch already holds. Every further tool cut there
# was cut and checked before its fold was decided, and each applies cleanly
# alone while any two of them fail in *both* orders:
#
#   A alone -> APPLIES      B alone -> APPLIES
#   A then B -> COLLIDES    B then A -> COLLIDES
#
# which is the silent failure shape `docs/findings/prepared-gate-patches.md`
# records -- each patch still applies on its own, so nothing a human does by
# hand catches it, and
# `tools/test_agent_gates_patches.py::CompositionTests` is what does. Each
# tool's own retention suite holds the halves of the call it added --
# `tools/test_gate_arm_audit_call_targets.py` and
# `tools/test_gate_arm_bank_attribution.py` -- which is the arrangement
# `ArmRetentionTests` holds for the disasm8051 patch's. **No count of them is
# written here**, and a later fold adds a call and a suite rather than editing
# a sentence: a header naming a number is a line every fold has to touch, and
# the count is what makes it one.
#
# #1081 asked for `docs/ci/agent-gates-bank-attribution-self-test.patch`, and
# that file was declined for the reason the measurement above gives, re-measured
# for this tool rather than inherited: `bank_attribution.py` reads a committed
# image, takes `<image> --self-test`, needs no Ghidra and no network, and wants
# this same tail, so its call folds here rather than shipping a file cut at an
# anchor that cannot compose with this one. The declined filename is beside this
# paragraph, per `CLAUDE.md`'s §4a-4d pattern, and `tools/check_doc_patch_refs.py`
# holds it as a name that is cited and deliberately absent from `docs/ci/`.
# **A branch adding one of these calls must serialise on this file**, rather
# than that being discovered at merge time; both write-ups say so.
#
# The filename is KEPT, and it names one of the tools rather than any of them
# alone, for the reference-count reason
# `docs/findings/prepared-gate-patches.md` gives for
# `agent-gates-capture-claims.patch`: the patch is named in prose across many
# markdown files outside `docs/ci/`, each of which names it as the canonical
# account of the prepared-not-landed shape, and renaming it would churn exactly
# the long shared files CLAUDE.md says not to churn.
#
# Original header, for ec/tools/reassembly_checked_bound.py alone:
#
# ec/tools/reassembly_checked_bound.py --check, wired into the cheap gate.
# Prepared here rather than landed, for the reason
# docs/ci/agent-gates-gap-text-check.patch gives: `.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh`
# is copied from the agent-pipeline template and the pipeline's push token has no
# `workflow` scope, so a branch editing it fails at the end of the PR rather than
# at the start of it.
#
# To land it:
#
#   git apply docs/ci/agent-gates-reassembly-bound-check.patch
#
# and that is the whole change.
#
# Why this one at all (issue #229). `verify_reassembly.check_one()` returned one
# count under two names: `instructions_checked` is how many instructions
# `to_sdas()` translated and handed to sdas8051, and how many of those bytes
# reached a comparison against the firmware image was not counted at all. That
# change made the tool carry both counts and split the two cases that both
# answered to `assembler-gap`; this puts a check on the committed report's own
# columns saying which rows compared nothing, so the corrected headline in
# `ec/ghidra/README.md` and `docs/findings.md` is re-derived on every commit
# rather than resting on a write-up. It reads `ec/ghidra/reassembly.csv` and
# `ec/decompiled/listing-index.csv` with the standard library's `csv` and the
# committed `.asm` listings, and nothing else: no assembler, no Ghidra, no
# network, no firmware.
#
# WHERE IT GOES, and why not into the tool loop it reads like it belongs in.
# Every position in that loop's tool list and every line of the `gate` list
# below is already another prepared patch's context:
# agent-gates-0751-self-test.patch and agent-gates-gap-text-check.patch both
# write into the tool list, agent-gates-disasm8051-self-test.patch rewrites its
# last entry, and capture-claims, pin-table-rows, testdata-row-claims and
# findings-frozen all write into the `gate` list or into check_doc_links(). A
# fifth hunk in either list cannot be applied alongside them in either order,
# which is what tools/test_agent_gates_patches.py fails on and what the
# findings-frozen patch's own header says about the gate list -- "composition,
# not tidiness". The tail of `check_ghidra_tooling()`, after the `done` and
# before the scratch directory is removed, is the one region no prepared patch's
# hunk reaches. It is the right gate regardless: every arm of that loop is a
# committed-text check under python3 with no assembler and no Ghidra, and this
# is one of those.
#
# COST, and the durable part is how *often* each call runs rather than what it
# costs. All three are tail calls after the tool loop's `done`, so each runs ONCE
# per sweep -- not once per entry in the tool list, which is what landing this
# block inside the loop body would cost, and what a re-cut that moved it above
# the `done` silently reintroduced.
# `tools/test_gate_arm_bank_attribution.py::PlacementTests` holds that relation,
# so a regression here goes red rather than multiplying the tier's cost.
#
# The per-call cost is the runner's, and the command that prints it is the one
# the gate itself runs, so no figure is written here:
#
#   time python3 ec/tools/reassembly_checked_bound.py --check
#   time python3 ec/tools/audit_call_targets.py ec/firmware/GMxMGxx_11.800 --self-test
#   time python3 ec/tools/bank_attribution.py ec/firmware/GMxMGxx_11.800 --self-test
#
# One runner's figures move by roughly a factor of two between runners -- the
# 0.15 s an earlier cut recorded here and the 5.9 s tier §14e's table carries are
# both this patch's *own* history and neither re-derives on the runner that reads
# it -- so the ratio was the durable part, which is the caveat
# `docs/agent-pipeline.md` item 6 gives for `call_graph.py` too. **None of these
# is the largest arm this gate carries**, which the earlier reasoning here
# claimed of `audit_call_targets.py`: `xdata_register_map.py --self-test` is the
# one that costs more, so these are material additions and not record ones. What
# makes the figures worth re-deriving anyway is that a cost figure that outlives
# its measurement is a claim nobody re-derives.
#
# ONE THING TO KNOW BEFORE LANDING IT, and the comment at the call site says the
# same thing where the next reader of the gate will see it. `--check` FAILS on
# what a commit can fix -- a cell that does not hold an integer, a `detail`
# shape it does not recognise, an anchor it could not read -- and PRINTS the
# overclaim rather than failing on it. That is the one deliberate departure from
# what the name suggests. The strict assertion would be red on the tree this is
# cut from, and would stay red until #157 re-reports `reassembly.csv` under the
# pinned nix build: a row whose assembled listing had no entry reports checked
# instructions and compared none of them under *any* build, because the
# committed CSV has no column that can tell the two apart yet.
# docs/ci/agent-gates-gap-text-check.patch records what happens to a gate that
# is red on the day it is landed -- "the cheapest way to make a gate get
# switched off" -- and that is the whole of the reasoning. `--fail-on-overclaim`
# is the strict form and is wired nowhere;
# `ec/tools/test_reassembly_checked_bound.py` holds it red on the committed
# tree, so it is not a flag nobody can tell works.
#
# **That polarity note is about `reassembly_checked_bound.py` only; the
# `--self-test` calls have the opposite one.** Each FAILS on the checks it adds
# here; each exits 0 on the committed image today, and a different dump that
# broke what its checks read would exit 1. So a reader landing this should know
# which call is which before concluding that a green run means the same thing
# for all of them.
#
# AND THE DOCSTRINGS MUST NOT CLAIM A PLACE HERE. `check_gate_arm_coverage.py`
# direction 3 reads a tool docstring that puts its tool *in* the gate and
# requires a `case` arm to satisfy it. None of these calls is a `case` arm --
# they are tail calls -- so a docstring claiming gate membership would go red
# against an arm that is landed, which is the opposite of what direction 3 is
# for. None of these docstrings names a gate path today, and that is what keeps
# the check green; the claim belongs here and in the write-up, which is where a
# reader comes to land the arm. Each tool's own retention suite holds it, using
# the checker's `claims()` predicate rather than a copy of it.
#
# What `reassembly_checked_bound.py --check` buys, and what it does not. It keeps
# the arithmetic of the corrected claim honest, and it is what fails if a row's
# columns stop supporting the reading -- which is what makes the correction
# falsifiable rather than a sentence nobody re-derives. It does not make the 1:1
# claim stronger, it does not settle why those rows' listings carried no entry
# (three candidates, all live, in
# `docs/findings/reassembly-checked-counts-comparisons.md`), and it reads no
# capture, opens no EC and reads back no register.
#
# What `audit_call_targets.py --self-test` buys, and what it does not. **It is
# not what first notices a different dump, and an earlier cut of this header
# said it was; that is retracted rather than left standing.**
# `ec/tools/test_audit_call_targets.py` is committed, is found by
# `tools/run-tests.sh`'s `test_*.py` sweep, runs in CI, and asserts the status
# `self_test()` returns over the committed image -- so both checks below are
# already red on a sweep that breaks either. Measured by mutation on the
# committed tree, forcing either check's condition false turns that suite red.
#
# What this call adds is narrower than the reasoning behind it claimed, and is
# the reason to land it: it runs the SHIPPED COMMAND as a process, where the
# suite calls `self_test()` in-process and so never goes through `main()`,
# `argparse` or the exit status. Two breaks are caught here and by neither other
# place, and **the transcript assertion is what catches the first of them**:
# break `--self-test`'s dispatch and the command exits 0 having printed the
# tool's own report with no `self-test passed` line on it,
# which an arm reading only `|| rc=1` calls green -- so the call greps the
# transcript for its `self-test passed` line as well as reading the status.
# Rename the flag and the command exits 2, which the status alone catches.
# `test_call_graph_gaps.py` asserts its subprocess's stdout for the same reason
# and its returncode alongside it; there, as here, the stdout assertion is the
# half that catches a suppressed transcript.
#
# What this call still does not catch, so that a green run is not over-read: a
# self-test that prints its summary line with a check deleted from it. This arm
# reads a status and a line, and so does the in-process suite; neither counts
# the checks. **That limit is the same for every `--self-test` call here**, which
# is why no arm below claims to gate a check count.
#
# The two checks `audit_call_targets.py`'s transcript carries, for a reader
# landing this: that the BL51 trampoline block's 403 entries are one
# 6-byte-stride run over `0x1150-0x1AC2`, and that all 170 relative sites
# resolving onto a trampoline entry have their own address inside the block, so
# none is a branch reaching it from outside. Both are measured over
# `ec/firmware/GMxMGxx_11.800` today and both name themselves when they break.
# **No second image has been tried and none is claimed** -- and the sweep would
# notice one breaking either, so the honest statement is about the arm's exit
# status, not about a dump nobody has run. It reads committed text and the
# committed image and nothing else; it opens no capture, reads back no register
# and settles nothing about the banking assumption `offset_for_runtime()` rests
# on, which is the tool's own opening paragraph and is not what these two checks
# are for.
#
# What `bank_attribution.py --self-test` buys, and what it does not. **An
# earlier cut of this header said this call "is not redundant with a committed
# suite" and that the pins, the stub decoding and the seed census "are proven by
# `ec/annotations/bank-attribution.md` and by nothing else". That was wrong, and
# it is corrected here rather than dropped: it had measured two suites and read
# the result as a census of the tree.**
# `ec/tools/test_bank_attribution_common_follow.py` is committed, is found by
# `tools/run-tests.sh`, runs in CI, and `TheSelfTest::test_it_passes` calls
# `self_test()` in-process over the committed image and asserts the status --
# so the stub decoding, the seed census and the hand-decoded pins, all of which
# are checks *inside* `self_test()`, are already asserted in CI. Measured by
# mutation on the committed tree, forcing one hand-decoded pin's condition false
# turns that suite red on `TheSelfTest::test_it_passes`.
# `ec/tools/test_dispatch_edges.py` imports the module and drives it as a
# subprocess, but only with `--regions-csv` and `--pairs-csv`.
#
# What this call adds is the same narrower thing the call above adds, not more:
# it runs the SHIPPED COMMAND as a process, where every one of those suites
# calls `self_test()` in-process and so never goes through `main()`, `argparse`
# or the exit status. Breaking `main()`'s `if args.self_test: return
# self_test(d)` leaves all of them green, and **the transcript assertion is
# what catches it**: the command exits 0 having printed the tool's own report
# with no `self-test passed` line on it, which an arm reading only `|| rc=1`
# calls green -- so the call greps the transcript for its `self-test passed`
# line as well as reading the status. That gap is what this arm is for.
#
# It still does not settle the banking assumption the tool exists to attack. The
# closure's four verdicts over both populations are re-derived from the committed
# image on every run, and that is arithmetic over bytes this repository commits;
# nothing here is evidence that the same-bank assumption is wrong on hardware, no
# capture was opened, no register was read back, and **no second image has been
# tried or is claimed**. A green run means the closure reproduced what it
# recorded; it does not mean the closure is right about the machine.

diff --git a/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh b/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
index 16c70c3..53c3d67 100755
--- a/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
+++ b/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
@@ -298,6 +298,113 @@ check_ghidra_tooling() {
         ;;
     esac
   done
+  # What the reassembly report says about its own columns: which rows whose
+  # detail reads `no bytes emitted at <addr>` compared nothing, and the ceiling
+  # that leaves for the instructions that did. issue #229 made check_one()
+  # count bytes compared rather than instructions handed over, and without this
+  # the corrected headline in ec/ghidra/README.md and docs/findings.md would be
+  # re-derived by nobody. Cheap tier for the reason every arm above gives: the
+  # committed report, the committed .asm listings and the standard library's
+  # csv. No assembler, no Ghidra, no network.
+  #
+  # `--check` fails on what a commit can fix -- a cell that does not hold an
+  # integer, a detail shape it does not recognise, an anchor it could not read --
+  # and *prints* the overclaim with its rows named rather than failing on it.
+  # That is deliberate and is the one thing to know before landing it: the
+  # strict form would be red on the tree it is landed onto, for a reason no
+  # commit to this repository can fix until #157 re-reports reassembly.csv under
+  # the pinned build, and a gate that is red on day one is the cheapest way to
+  # get a gate switched off. docs/ci/agent-gates-gap-text-check.patch records
+  # that happening once. `--fail-on-overclaim` is the strict form, wired
+  # nowhere.
+  #
+  # Called here rather than from the tool loop above, and the reason is
+  # composition: every position in that loop's list and every line of the
+  # `gate` list below is already another prepared patch's context
+  # (tools/test_agent_gates_patches.py holds them, and
+  # docs/findings.md's check_findings_frozen patch says the same about the gate
+  # list). This is the gate the tool belongs to either way -- it reads the same
+  # committed text every arm above reads -- and this is the one place in the
+  # file no prepared patch's hunk reaches.
+  if [ -f ec/tools/reassembly_checked_bound.py ]; then
+    python3 ec/tools/reassembly_checked_bound.py --check || rc=1
+  fi
+  # The BL51 trampoline block's framing, and the zero-external-sites answer
+  # that framing licenses: that the block's entries are one 6-byte-stride run
+  # over 0x1150-0x1AC2, and that every relative site resolving onto a
+  # trampoline entry has its own address inside the block, so none of them is a
+  # branch reaching it from outside.
+  #
+  # What this adds, narrowly: it runs the tool AS A COMMAND, which is the only
+  # place main(), argparse and the exit status are exercised. Both checks above
+  # already run on every sweep -- ec/tools/test_audit_call_targets.py is
+  # committed, is found by tools/run-tests.sh, and asserts the status self_test
+  # returns over the committed image, so breaking either one turns that suite
+  # red without this arm. An earlier cut of this comment claimed they "ran in no
+  # gate at all"; that was wrong, and it is retracted here rather than dropped.
+  #
+  # The exit status is not enough on its own, so the transcript is asserted and
+  # not merely printed. Break `--self-test`'s dispatch and the command exits 0
+  # having printed the tool's own report with no `self-test passed` line on it:
+  # the whole self-test stops running but nothing on stdout says so, and an arm
+  # reading only `|| rc=1` stays green on the one failure this call is here for.
+  # ec/tools/test_call_graph_gaps.py asserts its subprocess's stdout for the
+  # same reason, and it is the stdout assertion that does the work there.
+  # What neither this nor the in-process suite can catch is a self-test that
+  # prints its summary line with a check deleted from it -- both read a status
+  # and a line, and neither counts the checks.
+  #
+  # The tool takes a positional firmware path and no --work, so it cannot go in
+  # the tool loop above at all: on the `*)` default that is `--work "$scratch"
+  # --check`, and the tool's argparse exits 2 on those flags. That is
+  # check_gate_arm_coverage.py's direction 1, which runs in this same tier and
+  # would have gone red. ec/tools/audit_call_targets.py is committed text and
+  # the image is committed, so the cheap-tier reason holds either way. No
+  # Ghidra, no network, no assembler.
+  if [ -f ec/tools/audit_call_targets.py ]; then
+    python3 ec/tools/audit_call_targets.py ec/firmware/GMxMGxx_11.800 \
+      --self-test > "$scratch/audit-call-targets-self-test.txt" || rc=1
+    cat "$scratch/audit-call-targets-self-test.txt"
+    if ! grep -q 'self-test passed' "$scratch/audit-call-targets-self-test.txt"; then
+      echo "audit_call_targets.py --self-test printed no 'self-test passed' line" >&2
+      rc=1
+    fi
+  fi
+  # The bank attribution the closure's own verdict rests on, re-derived from the
+  # committed image on every sweep rather than on a PR body: the stub->bank
+  # decoding, the seed census, the hand-decoded pins, the jmp @a+dptr dispatch
+  # shapes, the four verdicts over both populations, and the bounds column.
+  # ec/annotations/bank-attribution.md's "Reproducing it" section is where a
+  # reader comes for what each of those asserts and why.
+  #
+  # Same shape as the call above and for the same two reasons. It takes the
+  # firmware as a positional path and neither --work nor --check, so the tool
+  # loop's `*)` default would hand it three flags its argparse refuses -- that
+  # is check_gate_arm_coverage.py's direction 1, in this same tier. And the
+  # transcript is asserted as well as read: break `--self-test`'s dispatch and
+  # the command exits 0 having printed the tool's own report -- the seeds, the
+  # per-bank closure table, the verdicts -- with no `self-test passed` line on
+  # it, which `|| rc=1` alone calls green, so the block greps for that line and
+  # cats the file so the transcript still reaches the gate log. That is the
+  # shape to expect: the fall-through prints a plausible report rather than
+  # falling silent, so nothing about the run looks broken.
+  #
+  # What it cannot catch, so a green run is not over-read: a self-test that
+  # prints its summary line with a check deleted from it. Both halves of this
+  # call read a status and a line, and neither counts the checks. No second
+  # image has been tried and none is claimed; a different dump that broke the
+  # closure's framing would exit 1 here, and the committed image exits 0 today.
+  # No capture, no register read back, and nothing about the banking assumption
+  # itself -- which is the tool's own subject and not what these checks are for.
+  if [ -f ec/tools/bank_attribution.py ]; then
+    python3 ec/tools/bank_attribution.py ec/firmware/GMxMGxx_11.800 \
+      --self-test > "$scratch/bank-attribution-self-test.txt" || rc=1
+    cat "$scratch/bank-attribution-self-test.txt"
+    if ! grep -q 'self-test passed' "$scratch/bank-attribution-self-test.txt"; then
+      echo "bank_attribution.py --self-test printed no 'self-test passed' line" >&2
+      rc=1
+    fi
+  fi
   rm -rf "$scratch"
   return "$rc"
 }
